#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167785} :activity/id #uuid "58280423-67e3-5aad-b53e-71bb3e1f83ef" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050188 :topic/id #uuid "34eb87b9-7036-55c0-88eb-1b1efbd11584" :topic/math-academy-id 3178} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167782 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168144 :tutorial/content "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the surface $S$ with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle xz + y,\\: x^2,\\: 2y-z^2 \\right\\rangle\\!, \n\\qquad \n\\mathbf{n}(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt5}\\left\\langle 2,\\: 0,\\: -1 \\right\\rangle\\!.\n$$\n\nLet's construct a surface integral representing the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}.$\n\nFirst, we recall that if $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨xz+y,\\,{x}^{2},\\,2y-{z}^{2}⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨2,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨xz+y,\\,{x}^{2},\\,2y-{z}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}((xz+y)\\cdot 2+{x}^{2}\\cdot 0+(2y-{z}^{2})\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(2xz+2y-2y+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2xz+{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral representing the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf n$ can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{2xz+{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{∬}_{S}2xz+{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can then evaluate this integral using the usual methods." :tutorial/id #uuid "c936101d-fbb9-594d-888c-2327d0908e71" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7632 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "88355efb-c547-50b1-b76e-a290fd494387" :step/math-academy-id 657078 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167783}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167783 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166880 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166485 :question/id #uuid "ac2a2137-53fa-5121-b541-02e0addd8757" :question/math-academy-id "e-12356"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21caaf9c-6b9b-5711-a434-21f0416e6c7e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048376}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421236} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421243}] :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Flux Integral as a Surface Integral"} :step/id #uuid "7c8b0ad8-3378-5dfc-8d4a-545a7208b294" :step/math-academy-id 657079 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167789}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167784 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166881 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166486 :question/id #uuid "fba5ed01-4c4c-50f6-8060-76bf40df79b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-12384"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3da3cc4f-0a1d-5639-b580-95eabc198d2a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050830}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167128} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278416} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323830} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421244}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Horizontal Plane"} :step/id #uuid "53d98073-01e7-5129-ad66-75d01c7d3e9d" :step/math-academy-id 657083 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362446}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167785 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168145 :tutorial/content "Recall that the flux of a two-dimensional vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)$ across a curve $C$ with respect to a unit normal vector $\\mathbf n(x,y)$ is given by the line integral\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nThe flux measures the total flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C,$ as depicted below.\n\n![](images/23/2392d6c27fc6767c78172936d6df37b3dfec8b2198abbdf9e22b87d4337ed9da.png)\n\nFlux is always measured with respect to a preselected unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and we have two choices of orientation for this vector. If we measure the flux relative to the unit normal pointing in the *opposite* direction, our flux will have the *same* magnitude but *opposite* sign.\n\n![](images/e4/e451548a5d9874b9eff9d61c2d423696dc76a3f560e2936eb7bdb3a9cbfbed49.png)\n\nThis lesson aims to extend the concept of flux to three-dimensional vector fields. But first, we need a brief discussion on surface orientations." :tutorial/id #uuid "8a17f47e-f50d-578f-a41c-9e537ded3620" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7725 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "507e9c0b-b2be-5bf9-a53c-e763d7e4315b" :step/math-academy-id 657075 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167787}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167786 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168146 :tutorial/content "In three dimensions, flux measures the total flow of a vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)$ *through an orientable surface.*\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)$ is a three-dimensional vector field, and $S$ is an orientable surface with the choice of orientation given by the unit normal vector $\\mathbf n(x,y,z),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/99/9976f35dcf348da89c15a40aad43ab5a1d3edea75558ef5539f9421ed9b75f4e.png)\n\n \n\nThe **flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $\\boldsymbol{S}$** with respect to $\\mathbf n,$ which we write as \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf S\n$$\n\n is given by the surface integral\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S  \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf n \\,\\textrm d S.\n$$\n\nIt's worth taking a moment to compare this to the flux formula for two-dimensional vector fields: \n\n- The quantity $\\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf n$ evaluated at a point $P$ on $S$ measures the projection of $\\mathbf F$ in the direction of $\\mathbf n$ at $P.$\n\n- By summing (integrating) the quantity $\\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf n$ over the entire surface, we get the total flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $S.$\n\nOur flux depends on our choice of orientation. If we were to select the *opposite* orientation, we'd get a result with the same magnitude but opposite sign." :tutorial/id #uuid "5a72f9c0-0bf4-59dd-be49-827f9e278fa3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7727 :tutorial/title "Flux in Three-Dimensional Vector Fields"} :step/id #uuid "4b9873f3-a548-5ea9-a86b-7682d0efa06b" :step/math-academy-id 657077 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167782}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167787 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168147 :tutorial/content "A surface is **orientable** if it has two distinct sides. So, for example, the surface $S$ shown below is orientable.\n\n![](images/29/29be1e415eaf0072aea7f4093e71665f5996b29804702a37a4d4493e0b0002d4.png)\n\n \n\nIf a unit vector $\\mathbf n(x,y,z)$ to our orientable surface varies continuously over $S,$ we say that $S$ is **oriented** and the choice of $\\mathbf n$ gives an **orientation** of $S.$\n\nWe can choose the opposite orientation for our surface by selecting a vector that points in the opposite direction, as shown below.\n\n![](images/40/40268c448b7c4ac99acf356e9f1e2702112a0d46dc1b6e6afd98d5c95e5ea317.png)\n\n \n\nWhen dealing with two-sided (i.e., orientable) surfaces, we must always be clear about our chosen orientation. \n\nNote that some surfaces are *not* orientable. This means they do not have two distinct sides. An example of a non-orientable surface is a Möbius strip, shown below.\n\n![](images/27/277ffb9e1594e64f3411b0c5063841616aaa433ccc4915bc96e2c9ab865ece95.png)\n\n \n\nA Möbius strip can be made by taking a long rectangular piece of paper, twisting one of its ends by $180^\\circ$ and gluing the two ends together. \n\nA Möbius strip does not have two distinct sides because if you were to start at a point $P$ on the strip and move along it (following the dark blue curve shown), you'd eventually end up at the same point but on the opposite side! Thus, a Möbius strip only has one distinct side. \n\nIn the work that follows, we will only consider orientable surfaces." :tutorial/id #uuid "e93caa6c-3516-5696-8b97-769b20ec4ac0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7728 :tutorial/title "Orientable vs. Non-Orientable Surfaces"} :step/id #uuid "15c5dca7-7c90-57e5-be91-60bf5e44e76b" :step/math-academy-id 657076 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167786}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167788 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166882 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166487 :question/id #uuid "35c3c9ba-60b0-5bcf-8e9c-2862ece44e7e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12454"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9a62365-4278-557f-ba76-caa31abe7869" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167129} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281492} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281494} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444619}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Tilted Plane"} :step/id #uuid "99f84857-19d8-532f-b1f8-974d1ed142df" :step/math-academy-id 657087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167789 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168148 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the horizontal plane $z=k,$ where $k$ is a fixed parameter. \n\nThe magnitude of the fundamental vector product is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}|| & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂k}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂k}{∂y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in such cases, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,f(x,y,z)\\text{d}S\\, & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,z)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,z)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRestating, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z)\\, \\textrm d S = \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y,z)  \\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nWe arrive at a similar result when considering vertical planes parallel to the $xz$- or $yz$-axes.\n\nThis observation can help provide a shortcut when calculating the flux of a vector field through a horizontal plane. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "3a45ce20-02a0-56e8-9b38-add2ba9480d1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7733 :tutorial/title "The Fundamental Vector Product for a Horizontal Plane"} :step/id #uuid "e8905e0e-c7f6-5708-9e74-d2d69c659271" :step/math-academy-id 657082 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167784}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362446 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362413 :tutorial/content "If we wish to find the flux through a tilted plane, we should first calculate the unit normal vector to this plane.\n\nFor example, consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle x,\\: 1-x+y,\\: 2y+8\\rangle$ and a finite region $S$ of the plane $z=2x+2y-1$ such that $\\textrm{Area}(S) = 2.$ \nAssume that we need to find the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $\\mathbf n.$\n\nIf $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is a continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z = 2x+2y-1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n\\langle x,\\: y,\\: z\\rangle \\cdot \\underbrace{\\langle -2,\\: -2, \\: 1\\rangle}_{\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}} = -1, \\quad \\textrm{or}\\quad \\langle x,\\: y,\\: z\\rangle \\cdot \\underbrace{\\langle 2,\\: 2, \\: -1\\rangle}_{-\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}}= 1.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\widetilde{\\mathbf n} = \\langle -2,\\, -2,\\, 1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,1-x+y,\\,2y+8⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,\\,1-x+y,\\,2y+8⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-2x-2(1-x+y)+2y+8) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-2x-2+2x-2y+2y+8) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}2\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "48c45790-f4c7-562f-9a3f-46bf6009dd4e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15974 :tutorial/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Through a Tilted Plane"} :step/id #uuid "81d0dc6b-4e76-5f80-934b-e300c50b0201" :step/math-academy-id 657086 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167788}}] :activity/title "Flux in Three-Dimensional Vector Fields" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166981}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444620} :activity/id #uuid "0bdff687-f8d4-5855-9373-b5ab9e4534b8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050188 :topic/id #uuid "34eb87b9-7036-55c0-88eb-1b1efbd11584" :topic/math-academy-id 3178} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444620 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167127 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51f5f2fd-94a6-51c3-ac48-99d59a93e907" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+2y+z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444605} {:answer/id #uuid "8524a039-125d-5c28-8aaa-2b417ce25d91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y+z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444606} {:answer/id #uuid "a0cc803d-28d2-5c8a-ad4d-e88ee6c149ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-y-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444608} {:answer/id #uuid "ad810e29-dc47-543e-aeb1-8297ce882407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444610} {:answer/id #uuid "e9761621-383d-53ec-8615-e110f65c1ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y+2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444613}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444610} :answer-field/id #uuid "63f86b8b-b32a-5de1-8bfa-b93d1b01899d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "44a48f57-f74b-5294-bb24-4cee5d89d1d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-161149" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with unit normal vector $n,$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨2z,\\,x+y,\\,y-z⟩\\,,\\,n(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩\\,.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{∬}_{S}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨2z,\\,x+y,\\,y-z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨2z,\\,x+y,\\,y-z⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(2z-(x+y)+(y-z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{z-x}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{z-x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{∬}_{S}z-x\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "03b4b191-1c32-5328-8c44-46701817acdf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444623}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444621 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444618 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23676b92-59ce-549e-bb44-0ce2c22aff60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444601} {:answer/id #uuid "30a14e60-74d3-59a0-9d7c-8bfb0dc4a3c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444603} {:answer/id #uuid "8c7915a4-09c6-54d8-8b86-d3abcf6997b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444607} {:answer/id #uuid "ca06b212-b30b-58a7-bceb-f7e5db8c00f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444612} {:answer/id #uuid "f6e593f7-c2c1-5fc5-8a3d-c699694f0c1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444614}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444614} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ac4cbf3-d6da-5e60-8eb4-cf067330135d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2ae7b222-1017-5e06-9f85-8323117b3184" :question/math-academy-id "q-162158" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z+5,\\,x-2y,\\,3x-2y⟩$ and a finite region $S$ of the plane $z=x+2y+1$ such that $\\text{Area}(S)=\\sqrt{2}.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=x+2y+1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=1,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince we require a downward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is negative. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=x+2y+1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z+5,\\,x-2y,\\,3x-2y⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨x+2y+6,\\,x-2y,\\,3x-2y⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}((x+2y+6)+2(x-2y)-(3x-2y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(x+2y+6+2x-4y-3x+2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0ad1dc8f-1c73-5500-a47a-c0fda9ab766e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444622 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278421 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2720bb2d-ef3c-5bd3-b5d1-47e2a36e5b59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "xy+yz+xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275834} {:answer/id #uuid "30f13b93-277e-5c15-b9c8-932581f5f992" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "xy-2yz+xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275912} {:answer/id #uuid "3b883b06-8332-53dc-bb7f-68fb90e59ae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3xz-yz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275997} {:answer/id #uuid "74a4ce69-d793-5db1-9424-5b3b421ad0a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276532} {:answer/id #uuid "8f857f74-c7bf-534b-90d2-dc5735bc9d12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "xy-yz+2xz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276777}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276777} :answer-field/id #uuid "74b1826a-ee9f-5272-9977-4058c1ea92ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fe4ccf73-c8f1-5e87-8e15-f27def60e911" :question/math-academy-id "q-161200" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with unit normal vector $n,$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨xy,\\,yz,\\,xz⟩\\,,\\,n(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,-1,\\,2⟩\\,.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{∬}_{S}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨xy,\\,yz,\\,xz⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,-1,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨xy,\\,yz,\\,xz⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-1,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(xy-yz+2xz) \\\\  & =\\frac{xy-yz+2xz}{\\sqrt{6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{xy-yz+2xz}{\\sqrt{6}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{∬}_{S}xy-yz+2xz\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8d884d62-3c79-5671-b709-2a062cef2457" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444621}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444623 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444619 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e7ca8ec-891f-5ccc-985b-4e4a85531ac2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444602} {:answer/id #uuid "3ccaaf5e-0e10-5c99-a0bc-d8a834cb86f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444604} {:answer/id #uuid "a5039a5b-f9d4-5a06-b32c-4be440d0e059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444609} {:answer/id #uuid "c73e0516-826a-57fd-99ad-0e6ddc957f95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444611} {:answer/id #uuid "f817a54c-d2b8-5a8f-9b28-dbefeb579916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444615}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444611} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c6a2aa9-7d14-5b93-966f-5ef76762e118" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "627a222e-9363-5b8b-b1ec-0730c89cfcbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-161193" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 3$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=\\sqrt{3}.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=x+y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=0,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=x+y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨x+y,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(-(x+y)-y+x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(-2y) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,y)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,y)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}y\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{3}}}y\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-2y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}-2y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-[{y}^{2}]_{0}^{1}\\cdot [x]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b60e2d93-3137-5009-9f82-8e66614f6b08" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444624}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444624 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401219 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "419c0982-db99-5289-b52f-a761ea3d1bae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2y-x-2{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401221} {:answer/id #uuid "d412c461-4939-5aa5-9ddd-df78a8e85bc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2y+{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401222} {:answer/id #uuid "a93a034f-199c-5c52-af3b-9d0a37596a31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y+{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401223} {:answer/id #uuid "dfbb580a-2ddc-5e43-a8f4-c46a6440cff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2y-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401224} {:answer/id #uuid "e9a49a16-7b19-549f-8e35-18199cc77301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+2{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401225}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401224} :answer-field/id #uuid "a21eed7a-f07f-5d01-b24f-3b6ba15f83ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401220}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dd68aee4-128c-55a1-8849-b847e40933c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-161198" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with unit normal vector $n,$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨{z}^{2},\\,x,\\,y-{z}^{2}⟩\\,,\\,n(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,2⟩\\,.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S=\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{S}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨{z}^{2},\\,x,\\,y-{z}^{2}⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨{z}^{2},\\,x,\\,y-{z}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\,-1,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2{z}^{2}-x+2(y-{z}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y-x}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{2y-x}{3}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{S}2y-x\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "de780f69-4606-5409-93bc-2ffc77e5cb9b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444622}}] :activity/title "Flux in Three-Dimensional Vector Fields" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444600}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536953} :activity/id #uuid "26d0a5d9-5b60-5aa6-b2c6-cedad250e8b6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050188 :topic/id #uuid "34eb87b9-7036-55c0-88eb-1b1efbd11584" :topic/math-academy-id 3178} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536930 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278420 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30c26cb1-a78b-546d-9346-a3292a576ab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "xy-3{e}^{z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275910} {:answer/id #uuid "751cc22b-1310-5836-99ad-05300271a000" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{e}^{z}+xy+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276537} {:answer/id #uuid "7e59aef0-444f-52b9-ac9d-43083f4f8de6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "xy-3-{e}^{z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276626} {:answer/id #uuid "e9be6646-27fa-5451-b505-31251d5e0a9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "xy+{e}^{z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277520} {:answer/id #uuid "fbfb7733-23eb-5b42-b8e0-72a48ba55123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "xy+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277687}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275910} :answer-field/id #uuid "902a8b32-2004-52a8-a453-ce38efbc95fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e0855a1e-4e9d-5aec-ac2d-5bafc7bc5253" :question/math-academy-id "q-161199" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with unit normal vector $n,$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨1-{e}^{z},\\,xy,\\,-3⟩\\,,\\,n(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}⟨3,\\,1,\\,1⟩\\,.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}{∬}_{S}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨1-{e}^{z},\\,xy,\\,-3⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}⟨3,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}⟨1-{e}^{z},\\,xy,\\,-3⟩\\cdot ⟨3,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}(3(1-{e}^{z})+xy-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{xy-3{e}^{z}}{\\sqrt{11}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{xy-3{e}^{z}}{\\sqrt{11}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{11}}{∬}_{S}xy-3{e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5d2db7f9-adcc-596b-a786-26e75633f558" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536982}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536932 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278416 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "163921df-9178-5c7c-84e9-a5ad409927f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275685} {:answer/id #uuid "4f29cce5-9391-5fc5-9827-e24070675f78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276183} {:answer/id #uuid "8f3e620d-f273-5944-9da0-c0e616000e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276776} {:answer/id #uuid "c9f0aacf-c8ca-535d-b816-ccbd1d5f8578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277272} {:answer/id #uuid "e91a3373-fcd8-5b74-860f-cfd0f567f1f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277516}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276183} :answer-field/id #uuid "0392007d-8d19-5f12-97f2-c3d7f67e5d76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277731}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d5ad771c-ffec-54eb-9e31-b5f8d4db3b56" :question/math-academy-id "q-161192" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨yz,\\,xz,\\,xy⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=4$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=4$ for  $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2,$ the upward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=4$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨yz,\\,xz,\\,xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4y,\\,4x,\\,xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}xy\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}|}_{-1}^{1}\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5f59b70c-7621-5b4b-9951-a03dc596f197" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536930}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536940 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281388 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ec4eec9-d885-5e59-95b5-8276383a1984" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278724} {:answer/id #uuid "a1df3b0f-3419-5642-80d6-43333a0f05d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280030} {:answer/id #uuid "cba2eb59-f72e-53a5-b7d3-0a0fb140ab74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280376} {:answer/id #uuid "d6581867-a9a5-5047-9e25-1ee87e16c985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280484} {:answer/id #uuid "e3fc824b-0743-5a05-ba40-9e5850d45c35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280603}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280376} :answer-field/id #uuid "364a17d3-0c49-5f9e-932e-cfdc2819fcb3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280930}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "88097b92-017e-5e6e-bb41-84bae76dd7ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-162154" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x+3,\\,x+y,\\,z-x+6⟩$ and a finite region $S$ of the plane $z=x-y$ such that $\\text{Area}(S)=\\sqrt{6}.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=x-y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=0,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=x-y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x+3,\\,x+y,\\,z-x+6⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨x+3,\\,x+y,\\,6-y⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(-(x+3)+(x+y)+(6-y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{6} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "76294a87-9654-50ec-a44d-a6393dfb7851"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536953 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278414 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f78e886-6217-5aab-ad45-03dff05fb9da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276186} {:answer/id #uuid "6e928e7b-dac4-58d8-8f43-244151a3df77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276469} {:answer/id #uuid "ddbf19ce-5145-539a-abcc-26cb05e19355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277420} {:answer/id #uuid "e1f62d61-7815-56c9-9362-400511988e3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277453} {:answer/id #uuid "ef9b9465-da83-54c2-a874-8ae563030fa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277582}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186277582} :answer-field/id #uuid "84c4e687-4548-5620-9905-d30fe986c526" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "79482994-a679-564f-a071-89c4e5f25898" :question/math-academy-id "q-161187" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨4y,\\,5x,\\,2z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=3$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $1\\le y\\le 3.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=3$ for  $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $1\\le y\\le 3,$ the upward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=3$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨4y,\\,5x,\\,2z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4y,\\,5x,\\,6⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}6\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =6{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\text{d}y{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot (3-1)\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9254b448-f46a-5f6e-bac5-fc3b31083f4a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536932}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536982 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421236 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2de3a55c-a3c2-5a9a-a812-bcdfecfb6feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+2y-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421180} {:answer/id #uuid "60427d20-92ba-5d11-ad42-138a2d763370" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421193} {:answer/id #uuid "69182493-5072-5c9f-a5f1-7e563c775c6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+3y+\\frac{z}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421194} {:answer/id #uuid "c4ece12f-0d28-5947-8017-589e0f4fd627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y+4z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421213} {:answer/id #uuid "f53c0e5f-6622-5e3f-8e8e-700ccb69b1fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3y-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421222}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421222} :answer-field/id #uuid "396f5c74-b8d5-5eee-bb2c-03c6070bb704" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3f3b4139-4781-5e1e-8511-61fe405f4cab" :question/math-academy-id "q-161202" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with unit normal vector $n,$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨\\frac{z}{2},\\,x,\\,z-y⟩\\,,\\,n(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{5}⟨4,\\,0,\\,-3⟩\\,.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S=\\frac{1}{5}{∬}_{S}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨\\frac{z}{2},\\,x,\\,z-y⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}⟨4,\\,0,\\,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨\\frac{z}{2},\\,x,\\,z-y⟩\\cdot ⟨4,\\,0,\\,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2z+0-3(z-y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3y-z}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{3y-z}{5}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{∬}_{S}3y-z\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ff2f0d5d-9693-56c0-8a11-c7a144fd0c57" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536940}}] :activity/title "Flux in Three-Dimensional Vector Fields" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536270}]}
